From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 16:14:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] HFR VRR: 1080p165 as the base where the sink lists it

The TV draws each picture at the base mode's speed: 8 ms on 1080p120,
5.6 ms on 1080p165, leaving the shutter glasses twice the time in which
the screen shows one eye. Where the sink lists 1920x1080p165hzV (the
kernel reads it from a DisplayID) and its VRR range reaches 165, HFR VRR
runs on it:

- vrr_rate is the rate itself, the largest even multiple of the film
  rate within 120 Hz, with "<rate> 1" for a 1000/1001 film (the mode has
  no 1000/1001 twin), so 23.976 fps plays at 95.904 Hz as before.
- If the mode cannot be set, 1080p120 is used, and 165 is not tried again
  until the sink's modes change.
- "...hzV" modes stay out of Kodi's resolution list unless
  /flash/vesa.enable asks for VESA modes, as before.

Leaving VRR writes vrr_rate 0 before vrr_mode none: the kernel takes a
rate only in game VRR, so the other order left the stretched line count
and the VRR packet behind.

Whether HFR VRR is still right for the playing film compared the film rate
with the one it was entered for as floats, exactly: a measured rate that
moved in its last digits (23.976, then 23.97602) counted as a new film and
forced a full mode switch in the middle of it. What a film rate makes of HFR
VRR (base mode rate, 1000/1001 twin, VRR rate and flag) is now worked out in
one place, fs3d_rates(), and the output counts as stale, or as matching
Kodi's resolution, by comparing that result, not the rate.

A hotplug while connected can read the VRR capabilities or the mode list
short for a moment, and that dropped frame-sequential support in the middle
of a film: a leave, a mode switch, and possibly another re-sync of the sink.
Support is now kept while 3D is on and the mode it runs on is still listed,
and otherwise dropped only when a second read agrees. A refresh also no
longer changes the base mode (120 or 165) while 3D runs on it; the next entry
takes the new one. The refresh records what the sink offered and each entry
reads it: one that came while 3D ran had nothing to set then, and nothing
refreshes on leaving, so the next film stayed on 120 until a hotplug.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/amlogic/AMLDisplay.cpp b/xbmc/windowing/amlogic/AMLDisplay.cpp
